\section{Experimental Setup and Metrics}\label{sec:method}

This section defines the benchmark, models, training procedures, and evaluation metrics that underpin the analyses in Sections~\ref{sec:fake_safety}--\ref{sec:avoidance_source}. The central methodological contribution is the displacement-filtered Collision Avoidance Rate (CAR$_\text{f}$), a corrective metric that separates genuine obstacle avoidance from action collapse (\S\ref{sec:dfcar}).

\subsection{Benchmark and Models}\label{sec:benchmark}

We conduct all experiments on SafeLIBERO~\cite{hu2025vlsa}, a collision-aware evaluation extension of the LIBERO simulation platform~\cite{liu2023libero}. SafeLIBERO augments standard manipulation tasks with per-timestep collision detection and non-target obstacle annotations, enabling systematic measurement of both task completion and safety. We select the \textit{object\_pudding} suite (Level~I), which comprises four tabletop manipulation tasks: pick \texttt{orange\_juice}, \texttt{chocolate\_pudding}, \texttt{milk}, or \texttt{bbq\_sauce} and place into a basket. In each task, non-target objects surround the manipulation target, and the MuJoCo physics engine registers any contact between the robot and a non-target object as a collision event.

We evaluate two Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models from the Pi0 family~\cite{black2024pi0}. \textbf{Pi0} (${\sim}$2.5B parameters) pairs a PaliGemma vision-language encoder with a dedicated action expert that generates continuous actions via flow matching~\cite{lipman2023flow}. \textbf{Pi0.5} (from Physical Intelligence) extends this architecture with broader pre-training data and serves as a stronger baseline with substantially higher task capability and less susceptibility to action collapse.

Table~\ref{tab:configurations} summarizes the six training configurations. Each model is trained for 30K steps. Evaluation uses 50 episodes per task across 4 tasks (200 episodes per configuration). For multi-seed experiments on Pi0.5, we run 3 seeds (600 episodes per configuration). Initial object placements are fixed across methods to ensure fair comparison.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Training configurations. Trainable parameter counts exclude the frozen backbone. The SDF auxiliary heads add $<$660K parameters to the ${\sim}$33.5M LoRA budget.}
\label{tab:configurations}
\vspace{2pt}
\small
\begin{tabular}{llr}
\toprule
Configuration & Description & Trainable Params \\
\midrule
Pi0 Full-FT & Full fine-tune, flow-matching loss & ${\sim}$3.5B \\
Pi0 LoRA & LoRA adapters only & ${\sim}$33.5M \\
Pi0+A1 (SDF aux) & LoRA + SDF decoder + EE extractor & ${\sim}$34.2M \\
Pi0+A3 (learned SDF) & LoRA + SDF decoder w/ gradient refinement & ${\sim}$34.2M \\
Pi0.5 LoRA & LoRA fine-tune on Pi0.5 & ${\sim}$33.5M \\
Pi0.5+A1 (SDF aux) & LoRA + SDF aux on Pi0.5, $\lambda \in \{0.005, 0.01, 0.1\}$ & ${\sim}$34.2M \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Standard Metrics}\label{sec:standard_metrics}

We report two conventional metrics. The \emph{Collision Avoidance Rate} (CAR) is the fraction of episodes in which no collision occurs between the robot and any non-target object:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:car}
\text{CAR} = \frac{|\{e : \text{safe}(e)\}|}{|E|}
\end{equation}
where $E$ is the set of all evaluation episodes and $\text{safe}(e)$ indicates that episode $e$ contains zero collision events. The \emph{Task Success Rate} (TSR) is the fraction of episodes where the manipulation task reaches completion:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:tsr}
\text{TSR} = \frac{|\{e : \text{success}(e)\}|}{|E|}
\end{equation}
These two metrics are standard in the SafeLIBERO evaluation protocol and in the broader VLA safety literature~\cite{hu2025vlsa,zhang2025safevla}.

\subsection{Displacement-Filtered CAR}\label{sec:dfcar}

Standard CAR conflates two qualitatively distinct behaviors: a robot that plans a trajectory around obstacles, and a robot that has collapsed into inaction. Both register as ``safe,'' yet only the former reflects collision avoidance capability. We introduce CAR$_\text{f}$, which excludes action-collapsed episodes from both numerator and denominator.

\paragraph{Action collapse.} We define an episode $e$ as \emph{action-collapsed} when its maximum end-effector (EE) displacement satisfies
\begin{equation}
d_{\max}(e) < \tau_d
\end{equation}
where $d_{\max}(e) = \max_{t} \| \mathbf{p}_t - \mathbf{p}_0 \|_2$ is the maximum Euclidean displacement of the EE from its initial position across all timesteps, and $\tau_d$ is a displacement threshold. We set $\tau_d = 0.1$\,mm, corresponding to sub-actuator-resolution motion.

\paragraph{Definition.} The displacement-filtered CAR is:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:car_f}
\text{CAR}_\text{f} = \frac{|\{e : \text{safe}(e) \;\land\; d_{\max}(e) \geq \tau_d\}|}{|\{e : d_{\max}(e) \geq \tau_d\}|}
\end{equation}
CAR$_\text{f}$ retains only episodes with sufficient EE motion and measures the collision avoidance rate exclusively among them.

\paragraph{Threshold robustness.} The specific choice of $\tau_d$ is not critical. The displacement distribution across all evaluated models is sharply bimodal: collapsed episodes cluster below 1\,$\mu$m, while active episodes exceed 200\,mm. No episodes fall in the intermediate range. This five-order-of-magnitude gap means that any $\tau_d \in [2, 200]$\,mm produces identical filtering results. We verify this empirically in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_threshold}.

\paragraph{Diagnostic capability.} CAR$_\text{f}$ serves two functions. First, it detects fake safety: Pi0+A1 reports CAR\,=\,91.5\% under the standard metric, but CAR$_\text{f} \approx 0$\% because 99.6\% of its safe episodes are action-collapsed. Second, it validates genuine safety: Pi0.5 achieves CAR$_\text{f}$ equal to its raw CAR (28.5\%), with 0\% of episodes filtered, because all safe episodes involve EE displacement above 288\,mm.

\subsection{SDF Auxiliary Training}\label{sec:sdf_aux}

We augment the LoRA-adapted VLA with two lightweight auxiliary heads that provide geometric supervision during training.

\paragraph{SDF Decoder ($f_\text{SDF}$).} A 4-layer MLP (hidden dimension 256, Softplus activation with $\beta = 5$, ${\sim}$395K parameters) predicts the signed distance $\Phi(\mathbf{x})$ from the current EE position $\mathbf{x}$ to the nearest obstacle surface. The decoder reads from intermediate features of the VLA action expert. The ground-truth signed distance field is precomputed from obstacle meshes using trimesh/pysdf, with values clamped at $\delta = 0.05$\,m and a safety margin $m = 0.01$\,m applied to shift the zero-crossing outward from the obstacle surface.

\paragraph{EE Extractor ($f_\text{EE}$).} A 2-layer MLP ($1024 \to 256 \to 3$, ${\sim}$263K parameters) predicts the 3D EE position from the same action expert features. This auxiliary head provides the query point for the SDF decoder and serves as an implicit regularizer on the learned feature representation.

\paragraph{Training objective.} The total loss combines the standard flow-matching action loss with the two auxiliary losses:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:total_loss}
\mathcal{L}_\text{total} = \mathcal{L}_\text{action} + \lambda \left( \mathcal{L}_\text{SDF} + \mathcal{L}_\text{EE} \right)
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{L}_\text{SDF}$ is a clamped $L_1$ loss on predicted SDF values with an Eikonal regularizer, $\mathcal{L}_\text{EE}$ is MSE on predicted EE position, and $\lambda$ controls the auxiliary loss weight. We evaluate $\lambda \in \{0.005, 0.01, 0.1\}$. The total auxiliary overhead is $<$660K parameters, less than 2\% of the ${\sim}$33.5M LoRA parameter budget.

\paragraph{Optimization.} All models are trained with AdamW, a cosine learning rate schedule decaying from $2.5 \times 10^{-5}$ to $2.5 \times 10^{-6}$, for 30K steps with batch size 32. For Pi0.5+A1, training proceeds in two phases: Phase~1 (steps 0--5K) trains only the auxiliary heads on detached features with the backbone frozen; Phase~2 (steps 5K--30K) unfreezes LoRA parameters for joint optimization.

\subsection{Oracle SDF Refinement}\label{sec:oracle_setup}

To test the theoretical upper bound of EE-only SDF refinement (Section~\ref{sec:ee_only}), we replace the learned SDF network with the ground-truth signed distance field computed from the simulation geometry. This eliminates prediction error and isolates the structural limitation of the refinement paradigm.

We evaluate three oracle variants:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textit{Oracle-adaptive}: runtime gradient refinement with a dynamic step size $\alpha$ that scales inversely with gradient magnitude, applied in Cartesian action space.
\item \textit{Oracle-fixed}: a constant step size $\alpha = 0.005$, also in Cartesian action space.
\item \textit{Oracle-feature}: gradient applied in the VLA's latent feature space rather than directly to Cartesian actions. This variant averages 241.7 refinement steps per episode (vs.\ ${\sim}$14 for the Cartesian variants), as the feature-space gradient must propagate through the action decoder.
\end{itemize}
Each variant is evaluated for 160 episodes (4 tasks $\times$ 40 episodes) with a single seed. The oracle experiments are designed to test Claim~2 (Section~\ref{sec:ee_only}): if EE-only SDF refinement is structurally sound, oracle performance should exceed unrefined baselines by a wide margin.

\subsection{The Fake Safety Problem}\label{sec:fake_safety}

At first glance, the collision avoidance results for SDF-augmented VLA models appear compelling. Our A1-LoRA model (Pi0 fine-tuned with an SDF auxiliary loss) achieves a Collision Avoidance Rate (CAR) of 91.5\% across 800 evaluation episodes on the SafeLIBERO benchmark~\cite{hu2025vlsa}, a figure that would suggest reliable obstacle avoidance. Yet this headline metric conceals a fundamental problem. When we examine the end-effector displacement of the 732 episodes classified as ``safe,'' the picture changes entirely: 541 episodes (73.9\%) exhibit a maximum displacement below 1\,$\mu$m, and another 188 episodes (25.7\%) remain below 0.1\,mm. Only 3 episodes (0.4\%) show displacement above the 0.1\,mm threshold, the minimum we consider consistent with intentional motion. An independent measurement confirms the diagnosis: obstacle displacement across these episodes has a median of ${\sim}$5\,nm, consistent with numerical noise rather than physical contact. The remaining 99.6\% of safe episodes are artifacts of a robot that never moved.

We define a safe episode as \emph{fake safe} when the maximum end-effector displacement stays below a threshold $\tau_d = 0.1$\,mm, the criterion used by the displacement-filtered CAR (Eq.~\ref{eq:car_f}). Under this definition, 729 of the 732 safe episodes produced by A1-LoRA are fake safe. An independent signal corroborates the diagnosis: TSR is 0\% across all 800 episodes, and the model produces no meaningful task-directed behavior. The robot did not avoid obstacles; it avoided motion altogether. The displacement distribution (Figure~\ref{fig:displacement}) clusters overwhelmingly near zero, with no spread toward values consistent with planned trajectories. Table~\ref{tab:fake_safety} summarizes the fake safety statistics across all evaluated methods.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Fake safety decomposition across methods on SafeLIBERO. CAR$_\text{f}$ excludes episodes with max displacement $< \tau_d = 0.1$\,mm. Fake safe rate is computed over safe (non-collision) episodes only.}
\label{tab:fake_safety}
\vspace{2pt}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Method & CAR (\%) & TSR (\%) & Fake Safe (\%) & Real Safe & CAR$_\text{f}$ (\%) \\
\midrule
A1-LoRA (SDF) & 91.5 & 0.0 & 99.6 & 3 / 732 & ---$^\dagger$ \\
LoRA baseline  & 82.0 & 0.0 & 99.4 & $\approx$\,1 / 164 & $\approx$\,0 \\
Pi0.5 & 28.5 & 71.5 & 0.0 & 57 / 57 & 28.5 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\vspace{2pt}
{\footnotesize $^\dagger$ Denominator $< 5$; statistically unreliable. Pi0.5 row from single-seed re-evaluation (seed~7, 200 episodes) with EE displacement recording; all safe episodes exceed 288\,mm displacement.}
\end{table}

This phenomenon is not an artifact of the SDF auxiliary loss. The LoRA baseline, trained without any collision avoidance objective, reports a CAR of 82.0\% with a Task Success Rate (TSR) of 0\%. Among its 164 safe episodes, 99.4\% are fake safe by the same displacement criterion. Pi0.5 presents a contrasting case. It is a larger model from the same family (${\sim}$3B$\to$${\sim}$3.5B parameters) that retains substantial task capability. Across three seeds (600 total episodes), Pi0.5 achieves a CAR of 26.8\,$\pm$\,1.6\% and a TSR of 74.7\,$\pm$\,1.5\%. Its lower CAR reflects genuine robot motion through the workspace, which naturally increases collision frequency. Re-evaluation with end-effector displacement recording confirms this distinction: across 200 episodes, all 57 safe episodes exhibit maximum end-effector displacement above 288\,mm; the fake safe rate is 0\%. Pi0.5's collision avoidance reflects genuine trajectory planning around obstacles, not inaction. Its CAR$_\text{f}$ equals its raw CAR (28.5\%), because no episodes are filtered by the displacement threshold.
For the Pi0-based methods (A1-LoRA and LoRA baseline), the conclusion is unambiguous: when these models do not collide, it is because the robot did not move.

These observations motivate the displacement-filtered CAR (CAR$_\text{f}$) defined in Eq.~\ref{eq:car_f}, which excludes episodes with sub-threshold end-effector displacement from both numerator and denominator. CAR$_\text{f}$ serves as a general-purpose diagnostic for the VLA safety evaluation community, applicable to any model and benchmark where per-episode end-effector trajectories are recorded.

The threshold $\tau_d$ admits wide latitude. Among episodes that do exhibit meaningful motion, the minimum observed maximum end-effector displacement is 239\,mm, three orders of magnitude above $\tau_d = 0.1$\,mm. Any threshold in $[2, 200]$\,mm produces identical filtering, because the displacement distribution is bimodal: episodes cluster either near zero (action collapse) or above 200\,mm (active behavior), with no intermediate population. We provide a full sensitivity analysis in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_threshold}.

For A1-LoRA, only 3 of 800 episodes exceed the threshold; all 3 happen to be safe, but a denominator of 3 precludes any meaningful rate estimate. For the LoRA baseline, CAR$_\text{f}$ is similarly near zero. Pi0.5 presents the opposite extreme: every episode exceeds the displacement threshold, so CAR$_\text{f}$ equals raw CAR (28.5\%). The gap between raw CAR and CAR$_\text{f}$ exposes the scale of contamination: A1-LoRA reports 91.5\% CAR but its CAR$_\text{f}$ is statistically undefined, because it has almost no active episodes to evaluate.

The practical implication is stark. Standard CAR evaluation conflates two behaviors that differ in every meaningful respect: a robot that plans a safe trajectory around an obstacle, and a robot that has collapsed into inaction due to distributional shift. Without displacement filtering, any model suffering from action collapse will receive inflated safety scores, and models that collapse more severely will paradoxically appear safer. This inversion renders CAR, as conventionally computed, unreliable for evaluating collision avoidance in VLA models. CAR$_\text{f}$ also serves a diagnostic role: a method that achieves a non-trivial CAR$_\text{f}$ value provides positive evidence that its safety behavior reflects genuine avoidance rather than inaction. Among all configurations evaluated in this work, Pi0.5 fine-tuned on collision-free demonstrations (Section~\ref{sec:avoidance_source}) is the first to produce a meaningful CAR$_\text{f}$.

\paragraph{Capability-safety observation.}
The fake safety pattern corresponds to a broader regularity in TSR-CAR space. Figure~\ref{fig:tsr_car_scatter} plots all evaluated configurations. Two clusters emerge. Pi0-family methods occupy the upper-left region: high CAR, near-zero TSR, with over 99\% of their safe episodes classified as fake safe; we term this the \emph{fake safety zone}. Pi0.5 sits in the lower-right: high TSR (73--76\% across seeds), low CAR (25--28\%). Its collisions reflect genuine robot motion through the workspace. The pattern holds across all Pi0.5 seeds and across Pi0 variants regardless of whether an SDF loss is applied. Models that execute tasks move the robot into collision-prone configurations; models that fail to act avoid collisions by default.

This spatial structure has a direct implication for metric interpretation. A model reporting high CAR but near-zero TSR provides no evidence of collision avoidance capability; its safety score is an artifact of inaction. CAR is informative only when conditioned on sufficient task capability. The TSR-CAR plane offers a more complete picture than either metric alone: the upper-right quadrant (high TSR, high CAR) represents genuinely safe and capable behavior, while the upper-left quadrant is accessible through action collapse alone. We report this anti-correlation as an empirical observation within two model families (Pi0 and Pi0.5), not as a universal law. A causal claim would require evidence across a broader range of architectures and benchmarks.

