\section{The Fake Safety Problem}\label{sec:fake_safety}

Standard CAR evaluation assumes that a collision-free episode reflects deliberate obstacle avoidance. This assumption fails when a model has collapsed into inaction: a robot that never moves will never collide, yet it has not avoided anything. We show that this failure mode dominates the safety scores of Pi0-family models and introduce CAR$_\text{f}$ as a corrective diagnostic.

\subsection{Action Collapse Contamination}\label{sec:collapse}

Our Pi0+A1 configuration (LoRA fine-tuning with SDF auxiliary loss) achieves a CAR of 91.5\% across 800 evaluation episodes, a figure that would suggest reliable obstacle avoidance. End-effector displacement analysis tells a different story. Of the 732 episodes classified as safe, 541 (73.9\%) exhibit maximum displacement below 1\,$\mu$m, another 188 (25.7\%) remain below 0.1\,mm, and only 3 (0.4\%) exceed the 0.1\,mm threshold. The remaining 99.6\% of safe episodes are artifacts of a robot that never moved. TSR is 0\% across all 800 episodes: the model produces no task-directed behavior whatsoever.

Applying CAR$_\text{f}$ (Eq.~\ref{eq:car_f}) filters out these action-collapsed episodes. Pi0+A1 drops from CAR\,=\,91.5\% to CAR$_\text{f} \approx 0$\%, because only 3 of 732 safe episodes survive displacement filtering. This is the primary demonstration of what CAR$_\text{f}$ is designed to detect: a headline safety score that is entirely attributable to inaction.

The contamination is not specific to the SDF auxiliary loss. Pi0 Full-FT reports CAR\,=\,83.0\% with TSR\,=\,0.5\%, and 99.4\% of its safe episodes are fake safe, yielding CAR$_\text{f} \approx 0$\%. Pi0 LoRA (without any collision objective) achieves CAR\,=\,82.0\%, TSR\,=\,0\%, with a comparable fake safe rate. Across all three Pi0 configurations, CAR$_\text{f}$ collapses to near zero.

Pi0.5 provides the validation case. Re-evaluation with displacement recording (200 episodes, seed~7) yields CAR\,=\,28.5\% and TSR\,=\,71.5\%. All 57 safe episodes exhibit maximum EE displacement above 288\,mm. The fake safe rate is 0\%, and CAR$_\text{f}$ equals raw CAR (28.5\%). Pi0.5 does not avoid obstacles by standing still; it executes tasks through the workspace and avoids collisions along the way. The displacement filter removes nothing, confirming that CAR$_\text{f}$ correctly preserves genuine safety.

\paragraph{Threshold robustness.} The choice of $\tau_d = 0.1$\,mm is not sensitive. The displacement distribution is sharply bimodal: collapsed episodes cluster below 1\,$\mu$m while active episodes exceed 200\,mm, a gap spanning five orders of magnitude. Any threshold $\tau_d \in [2, 200]$\,mm yields identical filtering results, because no episodes occupy the intermediate range. This bimodality makes CAR$_\text{f}$ robust to threshold selection rather than dependent on an arbitrary cutoff. We provide a full sensitivity analysis in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_threshold}.

Table~\ref{tab:fake_safety} summarizes the fake safety decomposition across all evaluated methods. Figure~\ref{fig:displacement} shows the end-effector displacement distribution.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Fake safety decomposition on SafeLIBERO. CAR$_\text{f}$ excludes episodes with max EE displacement $< \tau_d = 0.1$\,mm. ``Fake Safe'' is computed over safe (non-collision) episodes only. ``Real Safe'' counts safe episodes that exceed the displacement threshold.}
\label{tab:fake_safety}
\vspace{2pt}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccccccl}
\toprule
Method & Ep. & CAR (\%) & TSR (\%) & Fake Safe (\%) & Real Safe & CAR$_\text{f}$ (\%) & EE Disp (m) \\
\midrule
Pi0 Full-FT  & 200 & 83.0 & 0.5 & 99.4 & 1/166 & $\approx$\,0 & 0.027 \\
Pi0 LoRA     & 200 & 82.0 & 0.0 & ${\sim}$99 & ${\sim}$1/164 & $\approx$\,0 & 0.010 \\
Pi0+A1 (SDF) & 800 & 91.5 & 0.0 & 99.6 & 3/732 & ---$^\dagger$ & 0.003 \\
\midrule
Pi0.5 (3-seed)  & 600 & 26.8\,$\pm$\,1.6 & 74.7\,$\pm$\,1.5 & --- & --- & --- & 0.367 \\
Pi0.5 (reeval)  & 200 & 28.5 & 71.5 & 0.0 & 57/57 & 28.5 & 0.367 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\vspace{2pt}
{\footnotesize $^\dagger$\,Denominator $<5$; rate estimate statistically unreliable.}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\fbox{\parbox{0.92\linewidth}{\vspace{3cm}\centering\textit{Placeholder: end-effector displacement distribution.\\Bimodal histogram showing collapsed ($<1\,\mu$m) vs.\ active ($>200$\,mm) episodes.}\vspace{3cm}}}
\caption{End-effector displacement distribution across evaluation episodes. Pi0-family models (blue) cluster below 1\,$\mu$m, while Pi0.5 episodes (orange) exceed 200\,mm. The five-order-of-magnitude gap between the two modes makes the displacement threshold $\tau_d$ robust to any value in $[2, 200]$\,mm.}
\label{fig:displacement}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Capability-Safety Anti-correlation}\label{sec:anticorrelation}

The fake safety pattern corresponds to a spatial structure in the TSR-CAR plane. Figure~\ref{fig:tsr_car_scatter} plots all evaluated configurations. Two clusters emerge. Pi0-family methods (Full-FT, LoRA, Pi0+A1) occupy the upper-left: CAR between 82\% and 91.5\%, TSR at or below 0.5\%. Over 99\% of their safe episodes are fake safe. Pi0.5 sits in the lower-right: TSR of 73--76\% across seeds, CAR of 25--28\%. Intermediate configurations (Pi0.5+A1, LoRA-only fine-tunes) populate the middle of the plane, with TSR around 51--54\% and CAR around 54--57\%.

The pattern has a straightforward explanation within these two model families. Models that execute tasks move the robot through the workspace and into proximity with obstacles, raising the probability of collision. Models that fail to act remain near the initial configuration, avoiding collisions by default. Higher task capability correlates with lower CAR not because capable models are less safe, but because the denominator of risk exposure changes: a moving robot encounters obstacles that a stationary one does not.

We report this anti-correlation as an empirical observation within Pi0 and Pi0.5, not as a universal law. A causal claim would require evidence across a broader set of architectures, benchmarks, and task distributions. Within the scope of our evaluation, the observation reinforces a practical point: CAR is informative only when conditioned on sufficient task capability. The upper-right quadrant of the TSR-CAR plane (high TSR, high CAR) represents genuinely safe and capable behavior; the upper-left quadrant is accessible through action collapse alone.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig_tsr_car_scatter.pdf}
\caption{TSR vs.\ CAR for all evaluated configurations. Pi0-family models cluster in the upper-left (high CAR from inaction), Pi0.5 in the lower-right (high TSR with genuine collision exposure). Intermediate methods (Pi0.5+A1, LoRA-only) occupy the center. Error bars denote $\pm$1 standard deviation across seeds where applicable.}
\label{fig:tsr_car_scatter}
\end{figure}

