The fake safety pattern corresponds to a spatial structure in the TSR-CAR plane. Figure~\ref{fig:tsr_car_scatter} plots all evaluated configurations. Two clusters emerge. Pi0-family methods (Full-FT, LoRA, Pi0+A1) occupy the upper-left: CAR between 82\% and 91.5\%, TSR at or below 0.5\%. Over 99\% of their safe episodes are fake safe. Pi0.5 sits in the lower-right: TSR of 73--76\% across seeds, CAR of 25--28\%. Intermediate configurations (Pi0.5+A1, LoRA-only fine-tunes) populate the middle of the plane, with TSR around 51--54\% and CAR around 54--57\%.

The pattern has a straightforward explanation within these two model families. Models that execute tasks move the robot through the workspace and into proximity with obstacles, raising the probability of collision. Models that fail to act remain near the initial configuration, avoiding collisions by default. Higher task capability correlates with lower CAR not because capable models are less safe, but because the denominator of risk exposure changes: a moving robot encounters obstacles that a stationary one does not.

We report this anti-correlation as an empirical observation within Pi0 and Pi0.5, not as a universal law. A causal claim would require evidence across a broader set of architectures, benchmarks, and task distributions. Within the scope of our evaluation, the observation reinforces a practical point: CAR is informative only when conditioned on sufficient task capability. The upper-right quadrant of the TSR-CAR plane (high TSR, high CAR) represents genuinely safe and capable behavior; the upper-left quadrant is accessible through action collapse alone.

